  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CALP Network CORE, MEMBS, RESVS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The humanitarian system is under unprecedented strain. More than 300 million people are in need of assistance this year, and just 35% of the global humanitarian appeal has been funded. Food rations are being halved in refugee camps, and millions are experiencing catastrophic hunger. At the same time, humanitarian norms are fracturing, conflicts are escalating, and affected populations are increasingly hard to reach. This is the context in which the CALP Network operates. For 20 years, CALP has shaped and accelerated humanitarian response through cash and voucher assistance (CVA). Today, CALP convenes and connects more than 100 organisations  - NGOs, governments, academics, private sector innovators, and funders  - who share a commitment to putting people at the heart of crisis response. Cash should be the default modality of humanitarian assistance. Cash is faster, safer, more dignified, and better value for money than alternatives. It empowers people to make choices, strengthens markets, and ensures that limited humanitarian resources go further. The case for cash has never been stronger. In survey after survey, crisis -affected communities say cash is their preferred form of support. Cash transfers are proven to be cost -effective, scalable, and adaptable across crises  - from conflict to climate shocks. Innovations in digital payments, blockchain, and AI further expand the potential for secure, efficient delivery, even in the most fragile contexts.</narrative>
  </description>
